    body: `
<p>English changes the little word: "I ate <i>a</i> mango", "I ate <i>the</i> mango". Tagalog changes the verb. So far your verbs (-um-, mag-) made the doer the topic, marked by <b>ang</b>. That is <b>actor focus</b>. With an <b>-in</b> verb, the <i>thing</i> becomes the topic. That is <b>object focus</b>.</p>
<h3>Two ways to say "I ate"</h3>
{{ex:Kumain ako ng mangga.|koo-MAH-een ah-KOH nahng mahng-GAH|I ate a mango.}}
{{ex:Kinain ko ang mangga.|kee-NAH-een koh ahng mahng-GAH|I ate the mango.}}
<p>In the first, <b>ako</b> (the ang form) is the topic and the mango takes <b>ng</b>: some mango, any mango. In the second, the mango takes <b>ang</b>, and the doer moves to the ng form: <b>ko</b>. Only one noun per sentence gets <b>ang</b>.</p>
<h3>How the -in verb is built</h3>
<p>The completed form puts <b>-in-</b> after the first consonant of the root: <b>kain</b> → <b>kinain</b>, <b>bili</b> → <b>binili</b>. A root that starts with <b>l</b> takes <b>ni-</b> in front instead: <b>luto</b> → <b>niluto</b>. A root that starts with a vowel takes <b>in-</b>: <b>inom</b> → <b>ininom</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>basic</th><th>completed</th><th>ongoing</th><th>future</th></tr><tr><td class="k">kainin</td><td class="k">kinain</td><td class="k">kinakain</td><td class="k">kakainin</td></tr><tr><td class="k">bilhin</td><td class="k">binili</td><td class="k">binibili</td><td class="k">bibilhin</td></tr></table></div>
<p>The ongoing and future forms repeat the first syllable of the root, as you learned in the lesson on aspect.</p>
<h3>Who did it: the ng form</h3>
<p>The doer of an -in verb takes the ng form: <b>ko</b>, <b>mo</b>, <b>niya</b>, or <b>ni</b> before a name. The thing takes <b>ang</b>.</p>
{{ex:Binili ni Ana ang isda.|bee-nee-LEE nee AH-nah ahng ees-DAHQ|Ana bought the fish.}}
{{ex:Niluto ni Lola ang adobo.|nee-LOO-toh nee LOH-lah ahng ah-DOH-boh|Lola cooked the adobo.}}
{{ex:Kinain mo ba ang tinapay?|kee-NAH-een moh bah ahng tee-NAH-pai|Did you eat the bread?}}
{{ex:Iniinom ko ang kape.|ee-nee-ee-NOHM koh ahng kah-PEH|I'm drinking the coffee.}}
<h3>Which one to choose</h3>
<p>Look at the English. A particular thing ("the mango", "this fish", "Ana's bread") makes object focus natural: use the -in verb. "A mango", "some fish" or no thing at all: use the -um- or mag- verb.</p>
{{ex:Bumili ako ng isda.|boo-mee-LEE ah-KOH nahng ees-DAHQ|I bought some fish.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> mixing the two patterns. <i>Kumain ko ang mangga</i> → <b>Kinain ko ang mangga</b> (or <b>Kumain ako ng mangga</b> for "a mango").</div>
`,
